markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>Event → State → Response</h1>

<img src="https://images.code.org/917f3d8daa6b66a5a7eedf298da49e85-image-1761849785790.png" 
     alt="icon of hand pressing a button" 
     width="125px" 
     style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 20px 10px 0px;">
     
#### Now that you’ve experienced interactive features as a user, let’s zoom in on what’s happening behind the scenes in the code.

#### Most interactive apps follow a simple pattern: a user <strong>does something</strong>, the program <strong>tracks what changed</strong>, and the interface <strong>updates</strong>.
</p>

<br>



<h2>Chain of Events</h2>

<p style="font-size:14pt;">
This is the core logic behind interactivity in web apps:
</p>

<div style="display:flex; align-items:stretch; justify-content:center; gap:14px; flex-wrap:wrap; margin:18px 0;">

  <div style="background:#0093a4; color:white; border-radius:10px; padding:14px 18px; min-width:160px; text-align:center; box-shadow:0 3px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.12);">
    <div style="font-size:18pt; font-weight:bold;">Event</div>
    <div style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:6px;">A user action occurs</div>
    <div style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:8px; opacity:0.95;">(click, type, hover)</div>
  </div>

  <div style="font-size:26pt; font-weight:bold; color:#0093a4; display:flex; align-items:center;">→</div>

  <div style="background:#0093a4; color:white; border-radius:10px; padding:14px 18px; min-width:160px; text-align:center; box-shadow:0 3px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.12);">
    <div style="font-size:18pt; font-weight:bold;">State</div>
    <div style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:6px;">The program updates data</div>
    <div style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:8px; opacity:0.95;">(variables change)</div>
  </div>

  <div style="font-size:26pt; font-weight:bold; color:#0093a4; display:flex; align-items:center;">→</div>

  <div style="background:#0093a4; color:white; border-radius:10px; padding:14px 18px; min-width:160px; text-align:center; box-shadow:0 3px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.12);">
    <div style="font-size:18pt; font-weight:bold;">Response</div>
    <div style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:6px;">The UI reflects the change</div>
    <div style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:8px; opacity:0.95;">(text, color, layout)</div>
  </div>

</div>

<p style="font-size:14pt; clear:both;">
In other words: <strong>users interact</strong>, the program <strong>updates its internal “memory”</strong>, and the app <strong>shows the result</strong>.<br>
That loop is what makes a web app feel responsive and “alive.”
</p>




MARKDOWN
